# Atomic Design

Atomic Design is the primary architecture of Ctrl UI. It MUST be followed strictly. It is not a naming hint, a folder preference, or an optional guide.

Every public module MUST live in exactly one layer:

```
tokens → atoms → molecules → organisms → templates
```

- **tokens** — design decisions only (primitive → semantic → component tokens). No UI modules.
- **atoms** — smallest UI units. May use tokens only.
- **molecules** — simple combinations of atoms. May use tokens and atoms only.
- **organisms** — complex composed UI (tables, modals, forms). May use tokens, atoms, and molecules only.
- **templates** — layout shells and page-level structure. May use any layer below. Pages of consuming apps are out of this kit.

UI modules are **atoms / molecules / organisms / templates**. They MUST NOT be called “components” when the component-token tier is in scope.

Dependencies MUST flow downward only. A layer MUST NOT import a layer above it. A new file MUST be placed in its layer before it is written. A layer violation is a defect and MUST NOT merge.

Do not skip layers to “ship faster”. Do not start from organisms or templates. Grow the kit from tokens upward.

The first code increment is **toolchain + tokens only**. Atoms and later layers are the same product, later slices. Coverage and Storybook gates apply to public exports that exist. They MUST NOT be used as a reason to create later layers.
